                    <history>
                        <origin>
                            <origDate calendar="Gregorian" notBefore="1480" notAfter="1499">s.
                                xv</origDate>
                        </origin>
                        <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="org_893">Norton, Suffolk. Parish
                                church</orgName><p>East Anglian calendar, <q>Dedicacio ecclesie de
                                    Norton</q> added at 12 October. Obit of <persName>Nicholas
                                    Irby</persName>, 1467 (7 March). His will is at Norwich; he was
                                Rector of Norton.</p></provenance>
                        <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="org_813">Borley, Essex. Parish
                                church</orgName>
                            <p>The book, formerly in Suffolk, was at Borley in. s.xvi in. The
                                calendar contains the dedication of the church of Borley, and the
                                book has links to the Waldegrave family of Borley. See A. I. Doyle,
                                'Borley and the Waldergraves in the Sixteenth Century',
                                    <title>Transactions of the Essex Archaeological Society </title>
                                24 (1944) and 'Notes on a medieval kalendar (Downing Muniments IV)',
                                    <title>The Griffin</title> 43 (1946).</p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_LaterOwners"><p>In the nineteenth century, the
                                calendar (6 leaves) was detached by the Cambridge book-binder and
                                antiquarian, <persName>John Bowtell</persName>. In 1813, Bowtell
                                bequeathed this fragment to Downing College, Cambridge. The Downing
                                College fragment was later reunited with Cambridge, University
                                Library, Ee.5.13 (as noted in the Supplement at p. 73). See A. I.
                                Doyle, 'Two medieval calendars and other leaves removed by John
                                Bowtell from University Library manuscripts', <title>Transactions of
                                    the Cambridge Bibliographical Society</title> i
                            (1949).</p></provenance>
                    </history>
